<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of the Cosmin Forest</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1497">1497</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of the Cosmin Forest was fought on 26 October 1497 in northern Moldavia, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-the-great">Stephen the Great</a> ambushed the retreating army of the Polish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-albert">John I Albert</a> after his failed siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suceava">Suceava</a>, ending Polish attempts to control Moldavia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-cosmin-forest.jpg" alt="Battle of the Cosmin Forest" width="320" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juliusz Kossak · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cherubin_Gniewosz_in_the_battle_of_Suceava_1497.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1484 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilhorod-dnistrovskyi">Bilhorod-Dnistrovskyi</a> and Kiliia, the last Moldavian ports on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. Stephen the Great turned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a>, which sent 3,000 cavalry, and the joint army defeated the Ottomans at the Battle of Cătlăbuga. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV Jagiellon</a> won papal approval for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the Ottomans, and after his death his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-albert">John I Albert</a> took up the plan. He entered Moldavia claiming to help Stephen recover the lost forts, but his main aim was to put his brother Sigismund on the Moldavian throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Stephen asked his neighbours for help, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, Crimeans and Wallachians among them. John besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suceava">Suceava</a> from 24 September to 19 October 1497 while the Moldavians and their allies waged a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war on the Polish camps and supply lines, impaling Polish captives. Stephen gathered 18,000 Moldavians, 12,000 Transylvanians, 8,000 Wallachians, 5,000 Ottoman Akinji and some thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a>. After negotiations the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> left Suceava, apparently promised safe passage if they went home by the road they had come, but John chose another route through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a> towards Sniatyn.</p><p>The allies let the orderly Greater Poland column pass and struck the rest. The rear under Stanisław Chodecki held out, but as the baggage train entered the forest the Moldavians felled large trees onto it, and about half its guards were killed or taken. The unarmoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a> column, attacked from the left and pressed against rocky slopes, collapsed and lost about 7,500 men. A countercharge by the court companies under Jan Teczynski drove the allies back, but the pursuit failed and Teczynski was captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Polish army lost 11,000 men, a fifth of its strength, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> with it lost all their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>. The defeat dissuaded the king from challenging Ottoman supremacy on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. In 1498 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and Crimeans began raiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and sacking its cities, and in 1499 Poland sued for peace.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of the Cosmin Forest” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1484 Sultan Bayezid II captured Chilia and Cetatea Albă, cutting Moldavia off from the sea, and in 1497 Stephen beat a Polish invasion under John I Albert in the <b>Cosmin Forest</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
